A||||10||Standard||Halfshaft help..||||8||
Z||000000||Standard||08-31-2001||12:58 PM||||Opened the decklid today and noticed all these little grease marks on the underside of the decklid... I'm like "what the...?"  Then I keep looking down 'till I see the driver's side inner CV boot that's been split in two.  d'oh!<P>From what I've read, it's not that tough of a task to replace them.  Any pointers?<P>I'm guessing manual and auto half-shafts aren't interchangeable..  <P>oh yeah... how long do you think i can push my luck on this?  I'm not gonna have time to replace it until after the weekend, probably..  &lt;crosses fingers&gt;<BR>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||PBJ||08-31-2001||01:02 PM||||When you remove the axle to do it, check to see if your transmission axle seal has been leaking and replace it at the same time. also before you crimp the clamps-especially the large clamp, be sure there is no grease between boot and axle, and boot and clamp so it stays in place and does not slip off after a week of driving.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||08-31-2001||01:05 PM||||Well, I'm not going to rebuild it myself, I can't have my car down for that long.  So I'm going to order a rebuilt one from the Fierostore probably and just swap them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||08-31-2001||01:41 PM||||In the meantime, you can get a replacement boot that wraps around the joint. Just cut the old boot off and this one wraps around (so you don't have to remove anything).  You then fill with grease and you're on your way.  If the joint's ok, it'll give you some more life before you have to replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||08-31-2001||02:00 PM||||I actually just managed to finagle the old boot back on.  It looks like the metal band broke, instead of the boot splitting like I originally though.  Already stuffed some grease in there.  I think I'm gonna try a few wraps of bailing wire and some prayers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>And no hard launches anymore, either...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||08-31-2001||02:36 PM||||Just use a screw type hose clamp. If the boot is not split your good to go.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||08-31-2001||02:56 PM||||I would, but there's not enough clearance between the tranny case and the inner cv joint.  can't even get a finger through there.  This is an Isuzu tranny from a '96 sunfire with Fiero halfshafts.  Not sure if this is normal or not, though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||08-31-2001||07:50 PM||||Try a zip tie or two. <P>I didn't know you had a Sunfire transaxle. Does it use the Fiero mounts?||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||08-31-2001||07:59 PM||||When i pull Axles this is what i do:<BR>1. Remove Axle nut (30mm bolt)<BR>2. Remove Tire/Rim<BR>3. unbolt lower strut bolts (3/4" i belive)<BR>4. remove tie rod end bolt<BR>5. remove that bolt that connects hub thingy to A frame thingy<BR>6. Use crow bar to pry axle out<P>i would recomment changing axle seals but it sucks! very simple to do but you will cut up your hands bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P><BR>BTW do the spindle splines strip before the axles? 1 of my friends came over when i was workign on my Caddy engine and he would help me do somehting, so i told him to put my axles in, he did one fine but the other one didnt go in so he tryed forsing it by tighting it (found out later that was the wrogn axle) so i took it back and got the right axle and now this one will not fit on; any ideas?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-01-2001||12:16 AM||||Christ..... how many times does it need to be said... you can get most Fiero parts from any local parts store. Even if it's not in stock, most can get it same or next day and nearly always the same or less $$ then online ordering. Always less when core charges are involved. you've got a core plus return shipping cost then... If I buy local I get my core money back the same or the next day and I don't have to p_ss money away to UPS, FEDEX, etc, for return shipping.<P>Anytime you replace an axle, replace the transmission seal. Don't even think about skipping the seal.<P>I don't waste time with repairing CV's anymore. I just replace the whole shaft. If the boot is so old it's failed then other parts are on the way as well. Even if all other parts are good... it's too much of a pain in the ass to mess with CV's. I'm still finding grease from the last one that blewup over a year ago. And that's after I've pressure washed the area at least 5 times...||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||09-03-2001||09:28 PM||||Hrm, time to do this on the silver car.<P>Are the 'parts store' units genuine GM ?<P>If not, what's the best price source for OE GM complete halfshafts?<BR>Car is getting a V8, so second-best won't do.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Christ..... how many times does it need to be said... you can get most Fiero parts from any local parts store. Even if it's not in stock, most can get it same or next day and nearly always the same or less $$ then online ordering. Always less when core charges are involved. you've got a core plus return shipping cost then... If I buy local I get my core money back the same or the next day and I don't have to p_ss money away to UPS, FEDEX, etc, for return shipping.<P>Anytime you replace an axle, replace the transmission seal. Don't even think about skipping the seal.<P>I don't waste time with repairing CV's anymore. I just replace the whole shaft. If the boot is so old it's failed then other parts are on the way as well. Even if all other parts are good... it's too much of a pain in the ass to mess with CV's. I'm still finding grease from the last one that blewup over a year ago. And that's after I've pressure washed the area at least 5 times...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
